      "source_number": "1070",
      "actual_task": "There are6 locks and6 keys. Determine how many attempts can establish the correspondence between keys and locks.",
      "requested_answer_components": [
        "number of trials sufficient to establish the correspondence"
      ],
      "necessary_model": "For the standard pairing problem assume a fixed unknown bijection: each working key opens exactly one of the six working locks and each lock has exactly one of these keys. A trial tests one key in one lock and reliably reports opens/does not open. Determining a forced pair by deduction is allowed unless actual opening confirmation is explicitly required. The seed does not spell out these operational rules or worst-case versus favorable-case quantification.",
      "independent_solution_with_inference_allowed": {
        "result": "15 attempts guarantee determination, and15 is the minimum worst-case bound in the stated bijection/query model.",
        "constructive_upper_bound": "Take one unassigned key. Test at most five of the six unassigned locks. A success identifies its lock; five failures identify the sixth without testing it. Remove that key and lock. For the next key at most four tests identify its lock, then at most three, two and one. The final key/lock pair is forced with zero tests. Thus5+4+3+2+1=15 attempts suffice for every correspondence. Earlier successes can reduce the actual count.",
        "worst_case_lower_bound": "An adversary answers failure whenever a correspondence avoiding the queried pair still remains possible; it answers success only when every remaining correspondence already contains that pair. Such a success removes no candidate correspondence. At completion fix the unique remaining bijection and label its pairs key_i/lock_i for i=1,...,6. For each unordered pair i,j, at least one cross pairing key_i->lock_j or key_j->lock_i must have been ruled out by a failure. Otherwise swapping those two assigned locks would give a second bijection consistent with every failure and every forced success. There are6*5/2=15 unordered pairs, with distinct cross pairings for each, so this adversary requires at least15 failed queries. Any guaranteed procedure therefore needs at least15 total attempts in some case. This matches the upper bound.",
        "best_case_qualification": "Five successful tests of distinct assigned pairs can identify the sixth by inference. Fewer than five total queries cannot force a six-pair correspondence: if p successful distinct assignments are known, the remaining6-p keys still need at least(6-p)(5-p)/2 failures to rule out pair swaps. For total<=4, p+(6-p)(5-p)/2 is always at least5 for p=0,...,4. Thus the favorable minimum is5, not the worst-case15."
      },
      "if_each_correct_pair_must_actually_be_opened": {
        "minimum_guaranteed_count": "21",
        "derivation": "Try the first key until it actually opens a remaining lock: at most6 trials, then5,4,3,2 and1 for the rest. Total6+5+4+3+2+1=21. The same failure-when-possible adversary needs at least15 failures before complete assignment information, and actual confirmation requires6 successful pair openings, hence at least21 in some case. Favorable complete confirmation takes6 successful openings. This is a different operational objective, not a correction to15 under inference."
      },
      "source_qualification_and_hold": [
        "The source gives equal numbers of keys and locks but no explicit one-key/one-lock bijection rule. Equal cardinalities alone do not prove that compatibility is a bijection; without that rule, a unique six-pair correspondence need not exist.",
        "The source does not say every pair must physically be tested open, nor does it explicitly ask minimum guaranteed rather than favorable attempts. These distinctions change15 versus21 versus favorable5/6.",
        "Original intended operational/quantifier convention remains UNKNOWN/HOLD. The sharp15 guaranteed result is conditional and is not attributed to an unseen author key.",
        "The reliability and fixed compatibility of trials are model qualifications, not new facts recovered by consulting locks, images or other lessons."
      ],
      "source_status": "Complete optimal15 proof for inferred bijection,21 for full physical confirmation, and favorable counts; original operational convention not fixed."
    },
    {
