      "source_number": "1071",
      "actual_task": "Four balls have different masses. Determine how many weighings on a balance without weights suffice to arrange them in decreasing mass order.",
      "requested_answer_components": [
        "number of weighings sufficient to determine the decreasing order"
      ],
      "response_necessity": "No actual ball masses or prior comparison results are supplied, so a fixed named permutation cannot be the answer. A procedure that determines the order for every possible distinct-mass assignment certifies the count.",
      "independent_solution": {
        "minimum_guaranteed_weighings": "5",
        "constructive_sorting_procedure": "Use individual-ball comparisons. Weigh A against B and name the heavier/lighter pair H1,L1. Weigh C against D and name H2,L2. Third compare H1,H2 and interchange whole pair labels if necessary, so H1>H2, H1>L1 and H2>L2. H1 is globally heaviest. Fourth compare L1,H2. If L1>H2, the full order is H1>L1>H2>L2, completed in4. Otherwise H2>L1; fifth compare L1,L2, giving H1>H2>L1>L2 or H1>H2>L2>L1. Distinct masses mean none of these individual comparisons ties. This algorithm always completes within5.",
        "lower_bound_even_if_multiple_balls_may_be_on_a_pan": "Consider the admissible restricted family of masses{1,2,4,8}, assigned to the four labels in all24 permutations. Every two distinct subsets have different total masses because binary subset sums are unique. Thus any informative equal-arm weighing with disjoint balls on the pans has only the two possible directional outcomes on this family, never a balance tie. Empty/equal empty pans provide no information. A depth4 decision tree has at most2^4=16 informative outcome histories, but24 distinct orders must be distinguished. Therefore some assignment needs at least5 weighings, even when pooled-ball weighings are permitted. The5 upper bound is optimal for exact equal-arm comparison.",
        "why_a_ternary_information_count_is_not_enough": "A pooled weighing can balance for some arbitrary distinct mass quadruples, but distinct individual masses do not guarantee such an outcome or a useful third branch. The powers-of-two family rules out a universally successful four-weighing method; merely observing3^3>=24 does not construct a valid three-weighing sorter."
      },
      "qualifications": [
        "Uses the ordinary exact equal-arm balance interpretation, no external weights and no numeric force measurement or adjustable unequal lever-arm calibration. The source's balance noun is not promoted into a more powerful measuring instrument.",
        "The stated5 is the minimum guaranteed count. The source does not literally print the word minimum or worst-case; some favorable histories take fewer weighings. For example three individual tests A>B,B>C,C>D certify that particular decreasing order, but they do not guarantee it for every assignment.",
        "All4! label orders are allowed because only distinct masses are given. No expected mass distribution, equal spacing or known numerical masses is imposed.",
        "The sorting procedure and lower bound justify the requested count; they are not separate unprinted source subparts."
      ],
      "source_status": "Optimal guaranteed5 under ordinary exact balance rules; favorable versus guaranteed interpretation explicitly qualified."
    },
