    {
      "ordinal": 4,
      "source_number": "1073",
      "actual_task": "Alternately announce a running sum, starting with a number1 through10 and then adding one of those first ten numbers each turn. The person announcing exactly100 wins. Give a winning method and say which player wins under correct play.",
      "requested_answer_components": [
        "winning strategy",
        "first-player versus second-player winner under optimal play"
      ],
      "independent_solution": "The permitted increments are the first ten numbers1,2,...,10; the text does not say arbitrary real increments. The first player starts at1. After the second player adds k in1,...,10, the first adds11-k, also in1,...,10. Each opponent/first response pair adds11. The first player's announced targets are1,12,23,34,45,56,67,78,89,100. From89, the opponent can announce only90,...,99, and the first then reaches100 exactly. Thus the first player can force a win against every legal sequence of opposing additions.",
      "strategy_necessity_and_backward_check": "For a player about to move when the previously stated total is89, every allowed increment leads to90,...,99, from which the opponent can finish at100. This is a losing next-to-move position. By the same complement-to11 argument the totals1,12,...,89 are losing positions for the next player. The first turn from the initial total0 can reach the first such target by saying1. If the first instead starts at any a=2,...,10, the second adds12-a, a legal2,...,10, to take12 and thereafter follows the complement strategy to100. Hence1 is the unique winning opening within the permitted first choices.",
      "complete_result": {
        "winner_under_optimal_play": "first player",
        "opening": "1",
        "response": "Add11 minus the opponent's last increment; equivalently add the difference between the next own target and the newly announced total.",
        "target_totals": [
          "1",
          "12",
          "23",
          "34",
          "45",
          "56",
          "67",
          "78",
          "89",
          "100"
        ]
      },
      "wording_and_boundary_qualifications": [
        "The other player's addition is described as private, but the previous total and new announced sum determine that increment by subtraction. No secret-information obstacle is invented.",
        "Winning requires saying exactly100, not at least100. The source does not specify what happens if a player overshoots, but the forcing strategy never allows an overshoot on its reachable play path, so no additional overshoot rule is needed for this conclusion.",
        "All additions are positive and at most10. Replacing the stated first-ten-number move set with decimals, zero, negatives or arbitrary increments would be a different game.",
        "Correct play means each player seeks a forcing win; it does not mean the opponent cooperates with the first player's target choices."
      ],
      "source_status": "First player forces an exact100 win by1 then complement-to11; both requested strategy and winner covered."
    },
    {
      "ordinal": 5,
      "source_number": "1074",
      "actual_task": "A single shared piece starts at the lower-left corner of a chessboard. Each turn moves it one adjacent step right, up, or diagonally right-up. The player occupying the upper-right corner wins. Determine whether the first or second player wins under correct play.",
      "requested_answer_components": [
        "first-player versus second-player winner under optimal play"
      ],
      "independent_general_grid_solution": "Let r,s be the nonnegative numbers of remaining horizontal and vertical one-cell steps to the upper-right destination, on any fixed finite rectangular legal-position grid. Moves reduce r by1, s by1, or both by1, staying on the grid. A losing position for the player next to move is exactly one with both r and s even. From a nonterminal even/even position, every legal move produces at least one odd remaining count, so cannot reach another even/even position. From a position with just r odd, move right; with just s odd, move up; with both odd, move diagonally. The positive odd counts make these moves legal, and each reaches even/even. Since r+s strictly decreases, induction to terminal(0,0) proves the characterization.",
      "forcing_response": "First move to an even/even remaining position if possible. Then copy the opponent's direction on the next turn: right after right, up after up, diagonal after diagonal. The pair of moves reduces each used even count by2. Copying is legal because an opponent can reduce an even positive count only to an odd count at least1; the reply reduces it once more to an even count at least0. The opponent cannot finish directly from a nonterminal even/even position, and the responding player eventually makes the terminal move.",
      "conditional_board_results": [
        {
          "layout": "Ordinary8 by8 square-position board",
          "initial_remaining_counts": "7,7",
          "winner": "first player",
          "first_move": "One diagonal step, leaving6,6; then copy every opponent direction.",
          "other_openings": "A first right or up leaves6,7 or7,6; the second can move the odd coordinate to reach6,6 and take over the winning reply strategy."
        },
        {
          "layout": "9 by10 intersection-position Chinese-chess board",
          "initial_remaining_counts": "8,9",
          "winner": "first player",
          "first_move": "One upward step, leaving8,8; then copy every opponent direction. The8 by8 diagonal opening must not be silently substituted for this different layout."
        },
        {
          "layout": "General m-column,n-row position grid, not starting already at the destination",
          "initial_remaining_counts": "m-1,n-1",
          "winner": "Second if both m,n are odd; first otherwise.",
          "example_showing_size_dependence": "On a7 by7 position grid, initial6,6 is losing for the first, so the second takes the copy strategy."
        }
      ],
      "source_qualification_and_hold": [
        "The exact seed says only the board noun, no numerical dimensions, square-versus-intersection convention or selected figure/definition. Original numerical layout is UNKNOWN/HOLD; no imagined diagram or other lesson supplies8 by8.",
        "The first player wins under both common8 by8 square and9 by10 intersection readings, but the first move differs. The general parity theorem supplies the exact dependence if a nonstandard odd-by-odd layout were intended.",
        "The adjacent one-step language is retained. Rook-length/bishop-length jumps, backward moves, two separately controlled pieces or ordinary piece-specific chess movement are not introduced.",
        "The requested winner is supported by a strategy/parity proof; providing that proof does not turn it into an unprinted separate source strategy subpart."
      ],
