    },
    {
      "ordinal": 6,
      "source_number": "1078",
      "actual_task": "A chess tournament has only first-class players and masters, more than9 but fewer than25 participants. Every participant earns half of their own points against first-class players. Determine total entrants and the number of masters.",
      "requested_answer_components": [
        "total participants",
        "masters among them"
      ],
      "explicit_conditional_tournament_model": "Each unordered pair plays one game, no self-games; each completed game allocates a total of1 point to its two players, with a win1/loss0 or draw1/2 each. Points earned against first-class opponents include draw points. First-class/master labels do not impose a result or an aggregate score ranking. These are conditional rules, not printed facts in the seed.",
      "independent_conditional_derivation": "Let f be first-class players, m masters, n=f+m. The total tournament points are n(n-1)/2. For first-class participants, points against other first-class players sum to f(f-1)/2. The per-player half-share condition makes this half the first-class group's total score, so S_F=f(f-1). For masters, half their score is against first-class and the other half against masters; their internal games contribute m(m-1)/2, giving S_M=m(m-1). Thus f(f-1)+m(m-1)=n(n-1)/2, equivalent to(f-m)^2=n. Equivalently the cross-class games'fm points equal(S_F+S_M)/2=n(n-1)/4. Since10<=n<=24 and n is a perfect square, n=16. Then|f-m|=4 and f+m=16, so(f,m)=(6,10) or(10,6). Both master counts10 and6 remain algebraically admissible.",
      "complete_conditional_count_results": [
        {
          "total": "16",
          "first_class": "6",
          "masters": "10"
        },
        {
          "total": "16",
          "first_class": "10",
          "masters": "6"
        }
      ],
      "individual_feasibility_witness_not_just_aggregate_algebra": "For f=6,m=10 label first-class players F_i,i=0,...,5 and masters M_j,j=0,...,9. Make every within-class game a draw. In cross-class games, same-parity i,j draw; opposite-parity games are master wins. Each F plays5 internal draws for5/2 points and5 cross draws plus5 losses for5/2, so total5 and exactly half against first-class. Each M plays9 internal draws for9/2 and3 cross draws plus3 wins for9/2, so total9 and exactly half against first-class. Thus all16 individual conditions hold. Swap the two class labels in this same result table: because every participant had half their score against either group, all conditions still hold with f=10,m=6. This proves both count branches feasible, not merely necessary roots.",
      "why_tournament_format_cannot_be_silently_imported": "The source does not state that each pair plays once or specify point allocation. For a concrete non-round-robin countermodel, take12 entrants in three disjoint four-player groups, each with two first-class players and two masters. In each group play four drawn games forming a cycle: first-class versus first-class, first-class versus master, master versus master, and the other cross-class pairing. Each player has exactly one first-class opponent and one master opponent, earning1 point total with1/2 against first-class. All source number/half-score conditions hold with12 participants, not16. This shows why the standard-model conclusion is not unconditional without a schedule rule.",
      "source_qualification_and_hold": [
        "Original tournament format, draw/score convention and uniform opponent schedule remain UNKNOWN/HOLD. The full16 result is conditional on the stated pairwise constant-total-score model.",
        "Even under that model the data do not choose6 versus10 masters. A title/rating is not a printed inequality requiring masters to score more or outnumber first-class players. No legal count branch is suppressed to create uniqueness.",
        "The upper/lower participant bounds are strict:9 and25 are excluded, while the only interior square is16. Participant counts are integers because they count people, not an imported natural-number membership convention.",
        "Single-round-robin point normalization is harmless if all game scores are scaled by one common positive constant; the proof needs constant-total points and the stated schedule, not an unstated3/1/0 or unequal-round format.",
        "Constructing results above is an existence check for the complete conditional answer, not a separate original requested task or an authored diagram."
      ],
      "source_status": "Standard-model n16 and masters{6,10}, both individually feasible; original schedule/score and unqualified unique master count UNKNOWN/HOLD."
